good morning this is Steve here again  Steve Kaufman my blog is the linguist  blogs com of course I'm involved with  the language learning system called link  li and GQ com it's the morning here I  shaved yet i'm going to play hockey  later on with a group of old-timers so  I'm going to shave after my shower but  this morning had a very interesting  email from one of our Brazilian members  at link and of course in my previous  webcast I expressed my allergy to  grammar AZ this particular person put it  and I definitely do believe that grammar  teaching is largely counterproductive  and it has to do with the fact that we  learn from our experience and not  through some logical explanations that  are imposed on us but this person is a  teacher herself and she says well like  is there no role for teachers are you  getting rid of teachers so what is the  role of the teacher in different  situations first of all insofar as  language is concerned I'm quite  convinced that in most situations if we  simply let language learners listen to  things of interest read the same content  and then save words and phrases work on  vocabulary but experience the language  in following their own interests they  will learn at any age much better than  in any formal in class of instruction  now the role of teachers if I think back  to my years at school and university I  can only remember a few teachers and the  only teachers that I remember are the  ones that inspired me I remember nothing  that was taught to me but I remember  teachers that inspired me in high school  there was a teacher for example who got  a group of us to do a recording of Homer  The Odyssey and we recorded different  parts and he was so enthusiastic about  Greek mythology that he got us  enthusiastic quite a lot of us I'm not  just talking about two or three a lot of  us in the class got quite enthusiastic  about Greek mythology  on the other hand we had a French  teacher who was a reverend he wore black  robes we used to call him Zorro whenever  he walked into class we would sing the  theme from Zorro that was a popular TV  program but he was so cynical we  couldn't help picking on him all the  time so he did not inspire enthusiasm we  had a very enthusiastic Latin teacher  who would walk in the room and in those  days we have to stand up when the  teacher walked in the class and he would  draw up his books onto his desk and we  had to sit down before the books hit the  desk simple thing it kind of woke us up  and he was a nice guy and he was  enthusiastic and energetic my interest  in languages began when I went to  university and we had a teacher that  first year miserable time I still  remember him he's the one who got me on  the language learning because he made  french civilization so interesting it's  not what he taught us we got it out of  the books we got it out of I started  reading the local French newspaper i  lived in Montreal I didn't speak French  she even though I had French at school I  wasn't interested because we had a  series of we had one teacher in high  school who came in and he was actually  from England and he said all right you  guys now we're going to talk frog  language well I can tell you that did  not inspire us and so after all these  boring French teachers here was a person  who made french interesting and as a  result I went off on my own and started  learning French and from there I learned  Chinese and I learned a bunch of other  languages but he inspired me so my quick  answer is a teacher has to be able to  inspire the student number one role how  well the teacher has to be enthusiastic  and passionate about about something him  or herself and the teacher has to treat  the student as an individual not as some  kind of an unfortunate social welfare  case unfortunately if you'll be there is  far too much sort of social studies  involved in teaching you know the power  relationships gender relationships  unfortunate student because suffering  from whatever class racial ethnic gender  scrimmage forget all that stuff just  assume that the student has tremendous  potential and is an individual and needs  to be encouraged stimulated praised not  criticize necessarily but also we need  to tell students this this is reality in  life if you want a job you have to do  this how you achieve it is up to you  here are some resources I'm here to help  you but you have to do it on your own I  cannot teach you you can learn and here  are the things that I find fascinating  you may find them fascinating you may  not and maybe my personality will  inspire certain students at certain  students and maybe somebody else's  personality will inspire other students  but I think the role of the teacher is  to inspire to guide to provide feedback  to encourage to support etc not  necessarily to teach and that is  especially the case today when there are  so many resources available in various  media formats through the internet at  the library and so forth and I think  teachers should help your students  access this wider source of information  not hold themselves up as sources of  some great truth and that's especially  the case in grammar I mean people learn  through experience grammar is not some  great magnificent mathematical logical  structure it's full of inconsistencies  it varies from language to language  there's nothing wonderfully mathematical  about it at all it's just usage that's  evolved and has and will continue to  evolve and yes the students have to  identify that the pattern of users that  they want to imitate and they have to  imitate it in learning a foreign  language but it needn't be delivered  through the form of some great  scientific knowledge that the teacher  has that is going to be slowly drip by  drip and drop by drop delivered to the  student ok thank you there again so  that's my bit on the role of the teacher  I hope that satisfies are my letter  writer from Brazil  thank you bye-bye
 

